e found in the public repository [34]. VI Discussion In this work, we derive a finite set of inequalities that imply trumping. Specifically, we establish a finite set of conditions for trumping of state vectors under LOCC and general state transformation under thermal operations. Our results are also applicable to other restricted subsets of thermal operations known as elementary thermal operations and Markovian thermal operations, as the hierarchy of all such thermal processes collapses when catalysis is taken into consideration [35]. Moreover, the analysis presented here is general and we believe that can be non-trivially extended to other concepts, such as Matrix-majorization [36]. Our derivation builds on a set of inequalities relating ℓpsubscriptℓ𝑝\ell_{p}roman_ℓ start_POSTSUBSCRIPT italic_p end_POSTSUBSCRIPT norms to a specific polynomial, as done in [30]. We note that, the set of inequalities in [37] might be amenable to a similar result. The latter have a special significance in quantum information theory [38], as many of the basic entropic quantities have a much more elegant representation when defined over symmetric polynomials. It is interesting to compare the sufficient conditions derived here with other known conditions for the existence of catalyst under certain restrictions on the dimension of the latter. One such condition is given in [27]. There, the authors derive necessary and sufficient conditions for the 4444-dimensional vectors to admit a 2222-dimensional catalyst. It turns out that the set of conditions derived in our work (which works for vectors and catalysts of an arbitrary dimension) is incomparable to their condition. For instance, the example of a pair of 4444-dimensional vectors presented previously satisfies our computable criterion, but fails to satisfy the conditions of [27] thus implying that the catalyst needs to be at least 3333-dimensional. On the other hand, we present the following two vectors which satisfy the criterion of [27] and thus admit a 2222-dimensional catalyst, but in the same time do not satisfy our criterion: 𝐱=(0.46519,0.27313,0.20361,0.057807)𝐱0.465190.273130.203610.057807\mathbf{x}=(0.46519,0.27313,0.20361,0.057807)bold_x = ( 0.46519 , 0.27313 , 0.20361 , 0.057807 ), 𝐲=(0.46843,0.2693,0.20646,0.05581)𝐲0.468430.26930.206460.05581\mathbf{y}=(0.46843,0.2693,0.20646,0.05581)bold_y = ( 0.46843 , 0.2693 , 0.20646 , 0.05581 ). It is important to note that the conditions do not shed light on how to find the catalyst. In particular, we do not relate meeting the conditions with a bound on the catalyst dimension. Unfortunately, recent work hints at the computational untractability of this problem [39]. Acknowledgements. The authors thank Nelly Ng for useful discussions with an earlier version of this project. A.N. thanks Vjosa Blakaj for discussion of her work on level sets of entropy. S.S. was supported by the Royal Society University Research Fellowship. A.N. acknowledges support from MEXT Quantum Leap Flagship Program (MEXT QLEAP) Grant No. JPMXS0120319794 and ERC Grant Agreement No. 948139. A.N. also acknowledges support by the European Research Council (ERC Grant Agreement No. 948139) and the Excellence Cluster - Matter and Light for Quantum Computing (ML4Q). The authors declare no competing interests. References Marshall et al. [1979] A. W. Marshall, I. Olkin, and B. C. Arnold, Inequalities: theory of majorization and its applications, Vol. 143 (Springer, 1979). Mairal [2015] J. Mairal, Incremental majorization-minimization optimization with application to large-scale machine learning, SIAM Journal on Optimization 25, 829 (2015). Sun et al. [2016] Y. Sun, P. Babu, and D. P. Palomar, Majorization-minimization algorithms in signal processing, communications, and machine learning, IEEE Transactions on Signal Processing 65, 794 (2016). Nielsen [1999] M. A. Nielsen, Conditions for a class of entanglement transformations, Physical Review Letters 83, 436 (1999). Jonathan and Plenio [1